A||N||12||87GTBro||Code 32 --  EGR problem = dead car||||8||
Z||000000||87GTBro||02-15-2000||12:12 AM||||I recently bought an '86.5 GT V6.  Ever since I bought the car, after the engine would run at operating speeds for about ten to twenty minutes, the check engine light would come on.<P>I was fine with it because the engine never messed up (Even though the gas mileage was really low).  Well, I was just content with ignoring the check engine light until today.  After I had been driving the car all morning with no instances,  I stopped somewhere and came back to the car and it wouldn't start.  It would turn over, fire a few rounds and die.  It acted as if it were flooded even though it wasn't.<P>When I finally got home I checked the trouble codes on the ECM and I got a 32, which after doing a search I find out that it is the EGR and not the FGR like my Chilton's manual says.  Bad typo.<P>I found a loose hose going to the EGR solenoid and filter and I thought that took care of the problem.  I was wrong.<P>I had an interesting Valentines date with my girlfriend.  I picked her up in my car and it was running fine.  We started heading across town.  We were driving and I started noticing that the lights on the dashboard were getting dimmer.  The voltage on the voltmeter was slowly dropping.  I turned the A/C off and it jumped up a little.  Then it started dropping again.<P>To make a long story short, I shortened the date and headed home and made it to within 4 blocks of my house when my poor car died.  I checked the codes before I left my car in the dark for a romantic walk to my house.  It showed 32 and 44.  And I am sure the 44 is because of the 32.<P>I searched the Forum already.  Seems there have been plenty of problems with the solenoid.  How can you determine if your EGR problem is with the EGR valve or with the solenoid?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Standard||02-15-2000||12:20 AM||||Same car, same problems.  Just posted a new topic asking the same things.  oops.  I'm getting the same codes but my car hasn't died.  guess we'll make this a group effort!||||reg||11
Z||000002||mwbackus||02-15-2000||02:22 AM||||I have attempted to address some of these questions in the post that standard refers to above: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002341.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002341.html</A> <P>BTW, the loose vacuum line you found was probably connected to the intake of the EGR solenoid filter(large cylindrical end).  It has absolutely no purpose as far as I can determine.  I successfully removed this item from my engine and after a short contemplation, decided to kill it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||02-15-2000||02:31 PM||||87GTBro,<P>I don't think the car dying is because of the codes. It sounds like your alternator died. Could also be wiring though.<P>David||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTBro||02-15-2000||03:11 PM||||If the alternator died, I don't think the car would have made it back across town.  It would have drained the battery too quickly.  If it is the alternator, it is not all the way dead yet.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||02-15-2000||03:40 PM||||'87,<BR>the alternator sounds like a possible culprit. It doesn't have to be dead to cause problems. I had one go out on another car. It was putting out 8amps for a few days. Then finally died. I put 20 miles on the car with a completely dead alternator. Ran off the battery. The battery didn't show any major signs of drainage, but cranking was a little slower until it got topped off again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||02-15-2000||04:53 PM||||With a good battery a car can go a while with no alternator. One time I made it home about 100 miles on the interstate at night (lights on) in an 85 Shelby Charger with a dead altenator. By the time I got there the headlights looked like parking lights and it was running like crap... but it made it. <P>Yeah... I know... sounds like when your grandpa had to walk 10 miles to school... in the snow... uphill... both ways...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Something made the voltage drop while your car was running. That almost always means something is wrong with the charging system. (A VERY bad short to ground could cause the same symptoms but would most likely cause a lot of smoke and maybe a fire.) If it is not the alternator, then it could be loose wiring or even a loose belt. And yes, it could be an altenator that is still putting out a little, but not enough.<P>This is also not your ONLY problem. Finding and fixing this will just get you back to where you were before last night.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  There is some very good info on the code problems in the other thread mentioned above.<P>Good Luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||02-15-2000||05:08 PM||||I've never seen a code 32 make the engine stop running. I just started getting a code 32 (again), as soon as the car warms up. Every time. Just installed a new solenoid, last summer &lt;grumble&gt;.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-15-2000||07:19 PM||||Cars will commonly run many miles without the alternator. Longer w/o headlights and/or AC running. You can test the alternator, but unless the belt is gone the alternator is likely dead.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTBro||02-16-2000||12:17 AM||||The alternator was the culprit.  I replaced it today.  I had the old one tested and it was only dishing out 10 volts.<P>The new one cost quite a bit, but it works much better than the old one.  Thanks for your help.  Now all I have to do is fix the Code 32.  I get no vacuum to the EGR.<P>Are you supposed to have vacuum to the EGR at idle speed or does it only engage at high revs?||||reg||4
Z||000010||fiero56||02-16-2000||08:45 AM||||From what I've heard the EGR is only active when the throttle is opened up. Durring idle and WOT the EGR is disabled.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||02-16-2000||01:42 PM||||Exactly right.  At idle and wot the egr stays closed.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||DISenchanted||02-16-2000||05:06 PM||||You have all described my car as well.  I just bought an 88gt doint the same thing.  I can't make the temp sensor work though, be it the guage and the sender.  But my car died exactly as 87GTBro.  According to my volt guage, the alternator is fine.  I am replacing the rear mantifold and the EGR as son as it stops raining, the only thing that bothers me is that it still sputters on startup...<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
